  "label": "Learned Biomolecular Prediction Models",
  "related": [
    "networks-cycles-e4e1",
    "interactive-billions-1f20",
    "metabolism-proteomics-41aa"
  ],
  "secondary_categories": [
    "structural"
  ],
  "slug": "infection-dynamics-5c07",
  "summary": "Learned models over sequence, structure, and multi-omics data are now scoring functional specificity and calibrating metabolic predictions.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is learned models that score functional specificity from sequence, structure, and experimental inputs, although the items differ considerably in their inputs. WASP pairs AlphaFold structural homology with network topology for function annotation and reports higher F1 than sequence-based tools. PlantBGC applies a Transformer over Pfam-domain sequences, transferring microbial BGC training to plants through label-free masked language modeling. RED offers a cheap protein quality metric and shows that pLM training data curation governs access to metagenomic space beyond homology. ChemoCalib uses multiblock PLS across metabolomics, transcriptomics and proteomics to calibrate metabolic model bounds, improving 13C-MFA flux prediction over expression-only integration. ApexSP couples a signal peptide knowledge base with discrete diffusion generation and compatibility ranking for cargo-specific secretion design. What is new is the breadth of input modalities and label-free adaptation rather than a shared structural scoring method, since only WASP uses structures directly.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-07-31"
}
